# bigip-mgt-mfa demo configuration. Copy to .env (gitignored), fill in every <angle-bracket>
# value, then run ./deploy.sh. Full reference: docs/reference/configuration.md.
#
# TWO CONVENTIONS make this file readable:
#
#   1. A key that is COMMENTED OUT has a working default, and is shown WITH that default.
#      Uncomment only to override. Everything left uncommented you should read and decide on.
#   2. This file is SOURCED by every script, so it is shell, not an ini file. Any value
#      containing spaces must be QUOTED, or the words after the first are run as a command.
#      An unquoted <angle-bracket> placeholder is read as a redirect and fails the whole file
#      with "syntax error near unexpected token".
#
# Seven sections. Which ones you fill in depends on two choices:
#
#   1  this host + DNS                 always
#   2  directory — both modes          always
#   3  directory — BUNDLED only        only when MFA_DIRECTORY_MODE=bundled
#   4  directory — EXTERNAL only       only when MFA_DIRECTORY_MODE=external
#   5  BIG-IP                          skipped entirely by ./deploy.sh --stack
#   6  the second factor (TOTP)        always, all defaulted
#   7  certificates + rarely changed   always, all defaulted


# ═══ 1. THIS HOST ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# IP of the Docker host running the directory and DNS, as the BIG-IP reaches it.
# The generated certificate SANs are built from this, so it must match. CoreDNS binds
# this address specifically, not 0.0.0.0, to avoid colliding with a local stub resolver.
MFA_HOST_IP=<this-host-ip>

# ─── DNS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The demo ships its own resolver (CoreDNS), authoritative for MFA_DOMAIN, because the
# BIG-IP resolves in TMM through a dns-resolver that cannot read a hosts file — so a
# hosts-file fallback can cover your browser but never the BIG-IP. Everything that is not
# a demo name is forwarded upstream.
MFA_DNS_UPSTREAM=1.1.1.1            # where non-demo names go; no built-in default
#MFA_DNS_PORT=53                    # host port CoreDNS binds (change if :53 is taken)


# ═══ 2. DIRECTORY — BOTH MODES ══════════════════════════════════════════════
# bundled  = the demo runs its own OpenLDAP in Docker and seeds it (default; zero setup).
#            Fill in section 3, skip section 4.
# external = bring your own AD / FreeIPA / LDAP. The demo creates NOTHING in it and never
#            writes to it — APM binds to check a password and the BIG-IP reads memberOf.
#            Fill in section 4, skip section 3. See docs/directory.md.
MFA_DIRECTORY_MODE=bundled

# MFA_DOMAIN and BASE_DN must correspond (bigip-mgt-mfa.lab ⇄ dc=bigip-mgt-mfa,dc=lab).
# Bundled: MFA_DOMAIN also drives the OpenLDAP container and the LDAPS certificate SAN.
MFA_DOMAIN=bigip-mgt-mfa.lab
BASE_DN=dc=bigip-mgt-mfa,dc=lab

# Password of the ONE read-only bind shared by APM and the BIG-IP's system auth. It never
# needs write access and never reads userPassword — passwords are verified by BIND.
# Bundled seeds the account with this; external must match what the account already has.
MFA_BIND_PW=<change-me>

# ─── directory connection (live in BOTH modes) ──────────────────────────────
# These are NOT external-only: APM builds its AAA pool on MFA_LDAP_PORT and the BIG-IP's
# system auth uses MFA_LDAPS_PORT even against the bundled directory. The DN defaults are
# derived from BASE_DN, and the bundled seed is templated to match them.
#MFA_LDAP_PORT=389                  # plain LDAP (APM AAA bind)
#MFA_LDAPS_PORT=636                 # LDAPS (BIG-IP system-auth)
#MFA_BIND_DN=cn=bigip-bind,ou=svc,${BASE_DN}    # the read-only bind; password is MFA_BIND_PW above
#MFA_USER_SEARCH_BASE=ou=people,${BASE_DN}      # identity subtree — the only subtree in play
#MFA_LOGIN_ATTR=uid                 # becomes sAMAccountName when MFA_LDAP_SCHEMA=ad

# ─── who gets which BIG-IP role ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three groups, three TMOS roles, plus the default. Membership of one of these is the ONLY
# difference between the demo users. Authorization is decided by the TARGET BIG-IP's
# remote-role — not by APM — see docs/adr/0003-authorization-on-remote-role.md.
# Anyone who authenticates and is in none of them lands on guest (read-only).
#
#   bundled  : leave these alone. The seed creates all three groups under BASE_DN.
#   external : point them at groups that already exist in your directory.
#MFA_ADMIN_GROUP_DN=cn=bigip-admins,ou=groups,${BASE_DN}
#MFA_OPERATOR_GROUP_DN=cn=bigip-operators,ou=groups,${BASE_DN}
#MFA_AUDITOR_GROUP_DN=cn=bigip-auditors,ou=groups,${BASE_DN}

# How the BIG-IP tests each of those, as <attribute>=<value>. The default is real group
# membership (memberOf=<the group DN above>) — identical in both modes, which is what makes
# the AD path a configuration change rather than a different design. Override only if your
# directory cannot express membership that way.
#MFA_ADMIN_ROLE_ATTRIBUTE=memberOf=${MFA_ADMIN_GROUP_DN}
#MFA_OPERATOR_ROLE_ATTRIBUTE=memberOf=${MFA_OPERATOR_GROUP_DN}
#MFA_AUDITOR_ROLE_ATTRIBUTE=memberOf=${MFA_AUDITOR_GROUP_DN}


# ═══ 3. DIRECTORY — BUNDLED ONLY ════════════════════════════════════════════
# Ignored when MFA_DIRECTORY_MODE=external. Nothing here touches your own directory.
MFA_LDAP_ADMIN_PW=<change-me>       # OpenLDAP cn=admin password
#MFA_ORG=bigip-mgt-mfa              # OpenLDAP organisation name; cosmetic

# ─── demo principals: ONE PASSWORD PER USER ─────────────────────────────────
# A shared password makes per-user attribution rest on the honour system — a directory
# cannot tell two people apart if they present the same credential. Each principal's group
# membership is what selects its BIG-IP role.
MFA_PW_ALICE=<change-me>            # alice.admin  → bigip-admins    → Administrator
MFA_PW_CAROL=<change-me>            # carol.netops → bigip-operators → Operator
MFA_PW_DAVE=<change-me>             # dave.audit   → bigip-auditors  → Auditor
MFA_PW_BOB=<change-me>              # bob.user     → no group        → Guest (the default)
MFA_TEST_USER_PW=<change-me>        # fallback for any MFA_PW_* left blank


# ═══ 4. DIRECTORY — EXTERNAL ONLY ═══════════════════════════════════════════
# Ignored when MFA_DIRECTORY_MODE=bundled, where the first two are derived from
# MFA_HOST_IP and the generated CA. Validate the whole block BEFORE you touch a BIG-IP:
#   scripts/preflight-directory.sh
MFA_LDAP_HOST=                      # AD DC / LDAP address
MFA_LDAP_CA_FILE=                   # PEM of the CA that issued your LDAPS cert
#MFA_LDAP_SCHEMA=openldap           # openldap | ad (ad logs in as sAMAccountName)


# ═══ 5. BIG-IP ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Skip this section entirely for ./deploy.sh --stack, which contacts no BIG-IP.

# ─── unit A (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BIGIP_USER=admin                    # account the REST calls authenticate as
# Demo convenience only. PRODUCTION: inject from a secret manager rather than storing it
# here; the wrappers preserve an injected value across sourcing .env.
BIGIP_PASS=<bigip-admin-pw>
BIGIP_A_MGMT=<bigip-a-mgmt-ip>      # unit A management address (REST) — APM is built here
BIGIP_A_TMUI=<bigip-a-internal-selfip>   # A's non-floating internal self IP (SSO target)

# ─── unit B — a second BIG-IP (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# One unit is a complete deployment: one webtop tile, one system-auth pass, no sync. Most
# UDF blueprints and most home labs give you exactly one, and nothing about MFA at the
# management edge needs two. Leave BOTH blank for that.
#
# Set BOTH to add the second unit — its own webtop tile, its own remote authentication.
# Setting only BIGIP_B_MGMT is refused: a tile with no self IP behind it fails as a webtop
# link that hangs rather than as an error. A leftover "<sample>" counts as blank too — but
# QUOTE it, or sourcing .env dies on the redirect (see the header).
BIGIP_B_MGMT=                       # unit B management address (REST)
BIGIP_B_TMUI=                       # B's non-floating internal self IP (SSO target)

# ─── APM data plane ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MFA_APM_VIP=<apm-vip-ip>            # the webtop VIP users browse to (https://<vip>/)
MFA_WEBTOP_FQDN=webtop.bigip-mgt-mfa.lab  # name on the VIP certificate; browsers must trust
                                          # it, and CoreDNS answers for it. Keep it inside
                                          # MFA_DOMAIN or the demo zone will not resolve it.
#MFA_APM_TRAFFIC_GROUP=traffic-group-1     # float the VIP so the webtop survives a failover
# RFC 5737 TEST-NET façades standing in for each unit's TMUI. Non-routable on purpose;
# an iRule steers the real last hop. Keep them unless they collide with something you route.
#MFA_SHADOW_A=192.0.2.5
#MFA_SHADOW_B=192.0.2.6             # unused without BIGIP_B_MGMT

# ─── HA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#MFA_DEVICE_GROUP=                  # config-sync device group; empty = discover it via REST


# ═══ 6. THE SECOND FACTOR (TOTP) ════════════════════════════════════════════
# All defaulted. Seeds are enrolled with scripts/enroll-totp.sh.

# TOTP step in seconds. 30 is the universal default; 60 is gentler to type but NOTE that
# Google Authenticator ignores anything other than 30 and will silently produce wrong
# codes. FreeOTP, Aegis and 1Password honour it.
#MFA_TOTP_PERIOD=60

# Steps of tolerance either side of the current one. They MULTIPLY with the period: a code
# is accepted for period * (2*skew + 1) — 60s with skew 1 is three minutes. Set 0 for the
# tightest window (needs accurate NTP on the units); 1 is forgiving for demos.
#MFA_TOTP_SKEW=1

# Verifier throttling, per RFC 6238 §5.2. After this many consecutive wrong or replayed
# codes the second factor is refused — however correct the next code is — until the lockout
# passes. A success clears the counter. Six digits are only an adequate secret when guessing
# is bounded, so do not set this high.
#MFA_TOTP_MAX_FAILURES=5
#MFA_TOTP_LOCKOUT_SECONDS=300


# ═══ 7. CERTIFICATES + RARELY CHANGED ═══════════════════════════════════════
# Listed so they are discoverable without reading the scripts.
#MFA_CA_CN="bigip-mgt-mfa Demo CA"  # subject CN of the generated CA (QUOTED — it has spaces)
#MFA_LDAP_CA_NAME=                  # name of the CA object created on the BIG-IP; defaults
                                    # per mode (bundled: bigip-mgt-mfa-ca.crt,
                                    # external: bigip-mgt-mfa-dir-ca.crt)
#
# MFA_REGEN_CA is an operator ACTION, not a setting — do not put it in .env. Exporting it
# for one run mints a NEW CA instead of reusing certs/ca.crt, which invalidates every
# browser trust import and needs ./deploy.sh --bigip afterwards to reinstall the anchor:
#     MFA_REGEN_CA=1 ./deploy.sh --stack
#
# Also not a .env key, but part of the same story: TOTP seeds are AES-256 encrypted before
# they are written into the BIG-IP data group, under a key minted automatically into
# certs/seed-key.hex (gitignored, mode 600) on the first ./deploy.sh --bigip. Delete that
# file and re-deploy to rotate the key.
